{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_ground_state_upper","record":{"id":"scalar.source_ground_state_upper","kind":"claim","label":"Ground-state upper bound by every admissible Parisi value","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r2-13-source-guerra-upper","locator":"GroundState/WeakGuerra.lean:117–142; expectedMaximum_le_parisi; groundStateValue_le_parisi","statement":"For every W:BrownianSpace and gamma:OrderParameter, groundStateValue ≤ parisi W gamma. The preceding finite-spin theorem bounds expectedMaximum n by n * parisi W gamma for every positive n.","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar-r2-13-cite-ground-state-upper"],"boundary":"No minimizer or diffusion premise. The source passes a finite smooth-profile comparison through smooth coefficient approximation and then the already proved spin-size limit. Authored correspondence to inspected frozen source; unverified status is retained. No fresh Lean build, elaborated dependency extraction, axiom audit, Comparator acceptance, or independent mathematical acceptance is claimed."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:c941bb8e2efcb2c545fdebbfec6f904193275455192bec48bcb452c5b8ab7f97/nodes/scalar.source_ground_state_upper","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/69473417dc487cafa5e2e39b3168d2c4a8bffdad2e91b6e486de48f122dda071","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:b3fb22a55f09aba18f53cd9bbac859142355ee389bba5aa7bf98d016dc611a43/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:082c1187f897999ee934fa70dbb519221ed1e0aebc169fac91b7211a4a599dc5/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/formal-lower-overview","anchor":"formal-lower-overview","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:9e0f6d255eb0a116c513a0c11e94d28559f0a429ef365c3137bb4ff3b6a061d6/papers/long.md/sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json/passages/formal-lower-overview","anchor":"formal-lower-overview","mapping_ref":"sha256:7588abc48d1c21bb64c366849eaa851938d6acc6b17cf16f89ccf8b87ca26853/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"independent-scalar-lower-bound\"></a>\n\n### 10.2 A universal bound for predictable correlations\n\nThe scalar implementation obtains the value equality by two inequalities.\nIts finite-spin limit follows from superadditivity and a uniform upper\nbound. A finite-profile Gaussian comparison supplies\n$P_*\\le\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$ for every admissible coefficient. The reverse\ninequality at a minimizer comes from a more general finite-dimensional\nfact: predictable orthonormal Gaussian functions paired with any spin\nhave a shifted correlation sum at most $P_*$. We reconstruct that fact\nbelow. It supplies the inequality that §7's mesh limit consumes, without\ninvoking the literature's Parisi formula used in §6.\n\nWrite $e_n=n^{-1}\\mathbb E\\max_\\sigma H_n(\\sigma)$, with the Hamiltonian\nof §1. The finite-spin convergence and upper comparison are separate\ninputs to this subsection; their precise suppliers are\n[the superadditive limit][scalar-spin-limit] and\n[the upper comparison][scalar-upper]. The proof below concerns the\nlower comparison, not a finite-disorder algorithm or a circuit.\n\n**Proposition 10.1 (predictable Gaussian comparison).** Fix $K<\\infty$, independent standard normals $z_0,\\ldots,z_K$,\nand real functions $f_j=f_j(z_0,\\ldots,z_j)$ for $0\\le j<K$.\nAssume measurability, square integrability and\n$\\mathbb E[f_if_j]=\\delta_{ij}$. For any measurable\n$S=S(z_0,\\ldots,z_K)\\in\\{-1,1\\}$, the conclusion is\n$$\n\\sum_{j=0}^{K-1}\\mathbb E[f_jS]\\,\\mathbb E[S z_{j+1}]\\le P_*.\n$$\nThese assumptions allow nonconstant, dependent $f_j$; even\n$\\mathbb E f_j=0$ is unnecessary. Their order of dependence ensures\n$\\mathbb E[f_i z_{j+1}]=0$ whenever $i\\le j$. The next fresh coordinate,\nrather than centring of $f_i$, is what will remove the projection terms.\n[Scalar implementation, predictable-coefficient theorem][scalar-predictable].\n","span":{"start_byte":170048,"end_byte":171892,"start_line":3623,"end_line":3657},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=formal-lower-overview","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&paper=long&passage=formal-lower-overview"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_ground_state_upper&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_ground_state_upper&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_ground_state_upper&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=79bf16ac8ab3a2adf5741ab97b2e4bd813e1c0a1940efbbdf9e35523f00de8bb&node=scalar.source_ground_state_upp